test_fork — Phase 1 chunk 6 fork() end-to-end
==============================================

Drives `fork()` (SYS_FORK = 91) through the COW machinery wired
up in chunk 5: parent mmaps an anonymous page, writes a sentinel
into it, then forks. The child writes a *different* sentinel
into the same VA (which COWs into a private frame for the child),
calls `exit(0)`, and the parent waits on the child via PM and
verifies the parent's view of the page is still its original
sentinel — proving the two views were correctly decoupled.

What this test would fail-on if regressed:

  * `vma_clone_for_fork` not bumping refcount → child's COW would
    free the parent's frame mid-test, triggering page reuse and
    a spurious sentinel mismatch.
  * Forgetting to demote the parent's PTE to RO → child's write
    would silently update the *shared* frame and the parent
    would see the child's sentinel.
  * `arch_setup_fork_frame` not zeroing the return register →
    child would also see rc>0 and execute the parent branch,
    making both processes call `exit(0)` and the test would
    accidentally pass via a single-process write+read.
  * `kernel_reap_user_pages` not running on exit → child's COW'd
    frame would leak.

Pass criterion: prints `fork: ok`. The fail() helper exits with
the step number so a regression is greppable.

    module oskit.bin.test_fork

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, syscall6, pm_waitpid}


    val SYS_FORK = 91
    val SYS_MMAP = 222

    val PROT_READ  = 1
    val PROT_WRITE = 2

    val MAP_PRIVATE = 0x02
    val MAP_ANON    = 0x20

    val PAGE_LEN: i64 = i64(0x1000)


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("fork: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


    do_mmap(len: i64) -> u64
        val rc = syscall6(SYS_MMAP, i64(0), len,
                          i64(PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE),
                          i64(MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANON),
                          i64(-1), i64(0))
        if rc <= i64(0)
            return u64(0)
        u64(rc)


    do_fork() -> int
        int(syscall6(SYS_FORK, i64(0), i64(0),
                     i64(0), i64(0), i64(0), i64(0)))


    main(argv: []string)

Step 1 — mmap one anonymous page in the parent's mmap arena. No
fault yet; the VMA exists but the PTE is non-present.

        val page = do_mmap(PAGE_LEN)
        if page == u64(0)
            fail(1)
            return

Step 2 — write `0xAA` into the page from the parent. This is the
first touch: `vma_handle_fault` allocates a fresh refcount=1
zero frame, installs RW, and the store completes.

        var p: *byte = *byte(page)
        p[0] = byte(0xAA)
        if int(p[0]) & 0xff != 0xAA
            fail(2)
            return

Step 3 — fork. After this point the address space and the value
of `pid` differ between parent and child:
  * Parent: `pid > 0`  (the child's PID)
  * Child:  `pid == 0`
  * Failure: `pid < 0` — only the parent observes this.

The shared frame at `page` is now refcount=2, mapped RO in both
PTs.

        val pid = do_fork()
        if pid < 0
            fail(3)
            return

        if pid == 0

Step 4 (child) — write `0xBB` into the same VA. The PTE is RO so
the store traps; `vma_handle_fault`'s COW branch allocates a
fresh frame, copies in the parent's `0xAA`, installs RW, and
drops the child's reference on the original frame (refcount goes
back to 1). The store re-executes against the fresh frame and
writes `0xBB`.

            p[0] = byte(0xBB)
            if int(p[0]) & 0xff != 0xBB
                exit_with(20)
                return

Child terminates cleanly so PM's exit notification reaches the
parent's `pm_waitpid`. exit code 0 = "child saw isolation."

            exit()
            return

Step 5 (parent) — block on the child via PM. The child's exit
code is 0 if everything went well in the child branch.

        val rc = pm_waitpid(pid)
        if rc != 0
            fail(4)
            return

Step 6 (parent) — verify the parent's view of `page` is still
`0xAA`. The child's COW must have decoupled the two PTs; if the
child's store somehow landed in the original (shared) frame, the
parent would now read `0xBB` and we'd fail here.

        if int(p[0]) & 0xff != 0xAA
            fail(5)
            return

        puts("fork: ok\n")
        exit()
